        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Count!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Speaking: productive: Count out loud up to ten with help.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">interactive: Respond nonverbally to simple yes/no questions by nodding or shaking head.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Speaking: Can count up to ten out loud with teacher guidance (e.g., saying the numbers up to ten using a number chart).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can name familiar objects while counting (e.g., 'five pencils').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to questions related to numbers (e.g., responding to the prompt, 'Is this the number five?' with 'yes' or 'no').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads the class in singing the "Ten Little Fingers" song with accompanying TPR actions (holding up fingers, wiggling them). Students imitate the teacher, actively moving their hands and fingers while counting. This activates prior knowledge of numbers 1-10 and introduces the key vocabulary 'fingers' and the verb 'count' with attention to initial /f/ and /k/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up their own hand and slowly counts their fingers from one to five, clearly enunciating each number. "One finger, two fingers, three fingers, four fingers, five fingers." Then repeats with the other hand to ten. The teacher explicitly shows how to 'hold up' fingers and 'point' to each one. The teacher exaggerates the initial /k/ sound in 'count' and /f/ sound in 'fingers' while modeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to one finger: "Is this ONE finger?" (Students nod 'yes' or say 'Yes').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up two fingers: "How many fingers?" (Students say "Two!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards displaying numbers 1-10 and corresponding images of familiar classroom objects (e.g., one red block, three yellow pencils, five blue books, seven green crayons, ten orange markers). Realia: actual blocks, pencils, books, crayons, markers, glue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases aimed at the satisfaction of needs of a concrete type, such as identifying numbers and quantity of objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., showing 3 pencils). "Look! Three pencils! One, two, three pencils." Students listen and point to the number on the flashcard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard with a number (e.g., number 5). "What number is this?" (Students try to say "Five!"). The teacher explicitly introduces 'What number is this?' and 'How many?' questions, modeling the expected short answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up realia (e.g., two red blocks). "How many blocks?" (Students point to the number 2 on a chart or hold up two fingers).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice counting aloud using quantity + nouns (e.g., 'ten fingers', 'three pencils'), answering Wh-questions ('How many fingers?', 'What number is this?'), and Yes/No questions ('Is this number five?'). They also reinforce the initial /k/ and /f/ sounds and recognition of number sounds 1-10.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Counting & Object Naming (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher holds up flashcards with numbers 1-10 and corresponding objects (e.g., 'one red block', 'two yellow pencils'). Students chorally repeat the number and object name after the teacher. "One red block," "Two yellow pencils," "Three blue books." The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of numbers and object names.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. 'Show Me & Ask' (Semi-Controlled Substitution Drill):</b> The teacher calls out a number (e.g., "Show me four!"). Students hold up four fingers and say "Four fingers!" The teacher then asks "What number is this?" while holding up a flashcard, and students respond with the number. The teacher also asks "Is this number seven?" while showing number 7, and students respond by nodding/shaking head and saying "Yes" or "No." The teacher focuses on correct number vocabulary and the pronunciation of initial /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers' if they mention fingers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. 'Count the Objects' (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small bag with 5-10 mixed classroom objects (e.g., crayons, small blocks, erasers). Student A takes out a few objects and places them on the desk. Student B counts them aloud: "One, two, three, four pencils." Student A confirms or corrects. Then they switch roles. This activity encourages turn-taking and active listening, while practicing counting and naming objects.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group 'Counting Circle' Game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students sit in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has a set of number cards (1-10) placed face down. One student picks a card. They must 'hold up' that many fingers and 'count' them aloud. Then, another student in the group asks a 'How many?' or 'Is this...?' question related to the number. For example, if a student picks '5', they hold up five fingers and say, "One, two, three, four, five fingers!" Another student might ask, "How many fingers?" or "Is this number five?" The student who picked the card responds verbally. This game explicitly addresses the scenario "Hold Up Ten Fingers!" and the theme "I Can Count!" by engaging students in active counting and question-answering.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in counting aloud, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion (asking and answering questions about quantity). Students are encouraged to use the target vocabulary (numbers, hand, finger) and grammatical structures (Quantity + nouns, Wh-questions, Yes/No questions).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the 'Counting Circle' game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes each student during the 'Counting Circle' game as they:
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pick a number card and count aloud the corresponding number of fingers (or objects) up to ten.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond verbally or nonverbally to 'How many...?' and 'Is this...?' questions from peers or the teacher.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Can students pronounce numbers 1-10 clearly and the sounds /k/ and /f/?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Do students use 'hand', 'finger', 'block', 'pencil' etc., and numbers 1-10?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of counting:</b> Can students count accurately and with appropriate pace?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Do students participate in turn-taking and listen to others' questions/answers?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked to hold up a thumbs up if they can count to ten and show their fingers, and a thumbs down if it was difficult. The teacher asks, "Can you count to ten?" (Thumbs up/down). "Can you hold up five fingers?" (Students perform action).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was fun about counting today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What number is still a little bit hard for you to say?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students enthusiastically participated in the counting activities, especially the songs and finger counting. The 'Counting Circle' game was effective for practicing spontaneous speaking. Some students still need more practice differentiating between similar number sounds (e.g., 'three' and 'tree', 'six' and 'sick'). Nonverbal responses to yes/no questions were easier than verbal ones for some. For the next lesson, I need to ensure continued practice of number sounds and provide sentence frames for short verbal responses to Yes/No questions to build confidence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson provided ample oral practice with counting and identifying quantities. In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will transition from oral counting and identification to writing numbers and quantity expressions. They will practice tracing and writing numbers 1-10, and matching written numbers to pictures of objects. This will build on their auditory and visual recognition from this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>